tes "nerdiness" has broadened and become more mainstream (Marvel movies, gaming, etc.) So, how do I phrase this to validate the user's experience while clarifying my perspective? I want to say something like, "You make a fair point. The stereotype exists, and it's not without basis." Then I can explain that the initial statement was about the *monolithic* idea – the idea that *all* or *most* engineers fit that exact, often caricatured mold – becoming less accurate. I can bring in the points about the shift to collaborative work, the mainstreaming of tech, and the broader definition of "nerdiness". I can also bring back the advice given to the cousin, and restate that the overall advice remains valid, regardless of the presence of the stereotype. The field is big enough for people of all personalities. I want to come across as empathetic and objective. I need to avoid sounding argumentative or dismissive. Phrases like "That's a fair point," and "Here's why the perspective has shifted" will be useful here. I need to ensure the response remains respectful and helpful, without endorsing any negative generalizations as absolute truths. The goal is to provide context and help the user see why my initial response was phrased as it was, while also validating their perception.
